## Architectural Rationale

TAD Concepts intentionally adopts recognized security engineering principles as part of its Human-Centered Governed Orchestration (HCGO) architecture to:

- Improve system quality and reduce security exposure
- Support scalable enterprise design
- Enable future deployment into regulated environments
- Maintain architectural integrity across domain expansion

The architectural objective is **sound engineering** — not unsupported compliance claims.

This standard preserves architectural accuracy while avoiding unintended legal, contractual, or audit obligations.

---

## Relationship to HCGO Constitutional Architecture

This ADR is itself an expression of HCGO's constitutional governance model applied to TAD Concepts LLC as an organization:

- **Constitutional Guardrail** — explicit written requirement governing all language decisions
- **Approved language** — authorized outputs (parallel to authorized source material)
- **Prohibited language** — boundary detection (parallel to Component 106)
- **Exception handling** — governed process for edge cases (parallel to Domain Participation Contracts)
- **Audit trail** — this ADR as the governance event record (parallel to Component 138)

Constitutional governance applies not only to the platform — it governs the organization that built it.

---

## Consequences

### Positive
- Eliminates legal exposure from unsupported compliance claims
- Protects patent scope from compliance language that could narrow claims
- Preserves credibility with NSF, DoD, enterprise, and investor reviewers
- Establishes clear organizational standard for all future documentation
- Enables future compliance claims to be made precisely when earned

### Negative / Accepted Trade-offs
- Some marketing language may feel less authoritative without compliance labels
- Sales conversations may require additional explanation of "security-by-design" vs. certification
- Accepted: architectural accuracy outweighs marketing convenience

